@*
    Dashboard router.

    The framework ships WebsiteRoutes, which does the same assembly discovery. The dashboard
    keeps its own for exactly one reason: the 403 panel below is MudBlazor markup, and the
    kernel does not reference MudBlazor. An unmatched route is a status code and is handled by
    the branch's status-code re-execution; an AUTHORIZATION failure on a matched route is a real
    page, and this is what it looks like.

    Page assemblies come from ICurrentSite.Areas — which self-hydrates from WebsiteMountRegistry
    when the per-Site branch middleware has not run on the current scope. That matters here:
    Routes renders with @rendermode="InteractiveServer", so after the SSR prerender it hydrates
    inside a SignalR circuit, and a circuit never passes through branch.Use(...). Before the
    framework grew that fallback this file had to consult a dashboard-local registry instead, or
    every cross-assembly @page turned into "Not Found" the moment the page went interactive.

    ZonitRouteView honours [LayoutKey] / [NoLayout] / runtime ILayoutContext, so plug-in pages
    can @attribute [LayoutKey("Dashboard.Main")] without taking a project reference on
    Zonit.Dashboard.
*@
@namespace Zonit.Dashboard.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing
@using Zonit.Dashboard.Components.Layouts
@using Zonit.Extensions.Website
@using Zonit.Extensions.Website.Layouts.Components

@inject ICurrentSite Site


<Router AppAssembly="@typeof(DashboardApp).Assembly"
        AdditionalAssemblies="@AreaAssemblies">
    <Found Context="routeData">
        <ZonitRouteView RouteData="@routeData"
                        DefaultLayout="@typeof(DashboardMainLayout)">
            <NotAuthorized>
                <LayoutView Layout="@typeof(DashboardMinimalLayout)">
                    <PageTitle>Not authorized</PageTitle>
                    <MudPaper Class="pa-8" Elevation="0">
                        <MudText Typo="Typo.h2" Color="MudBlazor.Color.Error" Align="MudBlazor.Align.Center">403</MudText>
                        <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Align="MudBlazor.Align.Center" Class="mt-2">Not authorized</MudText>
                        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Align="MudBlazor.Align.Center" Class="mt-4">
                            You need additional roles or permissions to view this page.
                        </MudText>
                    </MudPaper>
                </LayoutView>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </ZonitRouteView>
        <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="@routeData" Selector="h1" />
    </Found>
    @* Router doesn't ship a <NotFound> slot for static SSR, but the routable
       NotFound.razor page (@page "/404") covers direct navigation. Unmatched
       runtime routes redirect there via PageBase's interactive handler. *@
</Router>

@code {
    // Area assemblies come from ICurrentSite, not from a dashboard-local registry.
    //
    // This used to resolve the mount from NavigationManager.BaseUri and look the assemblies up
    // in DashboardMountRegistry, because ICurrentSite was only populated by the per-Site branch
    // middleware and a SignalR circuit never passes through it. ICurrentSite self-hydrates from
    // WebsiteMountRegistry now — the same longest-prefix lookup, off the same BaseUri — so both
    // scopes get the same answer and the dashboard no longer needs a second registry to hold
    // what the framework already tracks. DashboardMountRegistry keeps only the per-mount data
    // that is genuinely dashboard-specific.
    //
    // The entry assembly is scanned by MapRazorComponents<DashboardApp>() separately, and this
    // component's own assembly is excluded because Router rejects duplicates with
    // "Assembly already defined".
    IEnumerable<System.Reflection.Assembly> AreaAssemblies
        => Site.Areas
            .Select(a => a.ComponentsAssembly)
            .Where(a => a is not null && a != typeof(DashboardApp).Assembly)
            .Distinct();
}
